<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
<title>Application Architecture Guide — ERP Unite</title>
<style>
  :root {
    --navy: #00395F;
    --navy-dark: #00253D;
    --navy-light: #1A5A8A;
    --cyan: #079FC3;
    --cyan-light: #4AB9D4;
    --teal: #00B294;
    --red: #F1554B;
    --yellow: #F2B949;
    --slate: #44546A;
    --mute: #737373;
    --bg: #FAFBFC;
    --card-bg: #FFFFFF;
    --border: #E5E9EE;
    --code-bg: #F4F7FA;
    --code-border: #DCE3EB;
  }

  * { margin: 0; padding: 0; box-sizing: border-box; }

  html { scroll-behavior: smooth; }

  body {
    font-family: 'Segoe UI', -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, sans-serif;
    color: var(--navy);
    background: var(--bg);
    line-height: 1.6;
    -webkit-font-smoothing: antialiased;
  }

  /* ─── Hero ─── */

  .doc-hero {
    background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--navy-dark) 0%, var(--navy) 50%, var(--navy-light) 100%);
    color: white;
    padding: 56px 40px 64px;
    position: relative;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .doc-hero::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    top: -40%;
    right: -10%;
    width: 500px;
    height: 500px;
    background: radial-gradient(circle, rgba(7, 159, 195, 0.22) 0%, transparent 65%);
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .doc-hero::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    bottom: 0;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    height: 4px;
    background: linear-gradient(90deg, var(--cyan) 0%, var(--teal) 50%, var(--cyan) 100%);
  }

  .doc-hero-inner {
    max-width: 960px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    position: relative;
  }

  .doc-eyebrow {
    color: var(--cyan-light);
    font-size: 13px;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 2px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    margin-bottom: 12px;
  }

  .doc-eyebrow a {
    color: var(--cyan-light);
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px dotted rgba(74, 185, 212, 0.5);
    padding-bottom: 2px;
    transition: border-color 0.2s ease;
  }

  .doc-eyebrow a:hover { border-color: var(--cyan-light); }

  .doc-hero h1 {
    font-size: 48px;
    font-weight: 400;
    letter-spacing: -0.5px;
    line-height: 1.1;
    margin-bottom: 14px;
  }

  .doc-hero .subtitle {
    font-size: 18px;
    font-weight: 300;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.78);
    max-width: 720px;
  }

  /* ─── Body ─── */

  main {
    max-width: 960px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 48px 40px 80px;
  }

  main h1, main h2, main h3, main h4, main h5, main h6 {
    color: var(--navy);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.25;
    margin-top: 2em;
    margin-bottom: 0.6em;
  }

  main h1 { font-size: 32px; border-bottom: 2px solid var(--cyan); padding-bottom: 8px; margin-top: 1.6em; }
  main h2 { font-size: 24px; color: var(--navy); margin-top: 2em; }
  main h2::before {
    content: '';
    display: inline-block;
    width: 4px;
    height: 20px;
    background: var(--cyan);
    margin-right: 12px;
    vertical-align: middle;
    border-radius: 2px;
  }
  main h3 { font-size: 19px; color: var(--navy-light); }
  main h4 { font-size: 16px; color: var(--slate); text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 1px; }
  main h5, main h6 { font-size: 15px; color: var(--slate); }

  main p, main li { font-size: 16px; color: #1F2D3D; line-height: 1.7; }
  main p { margin-bottom: 1em; }

  main ul, main ol {
    margin-left: 1.5em;
    margin-bottom: 1em;
  }

  main ul li, main ol li { margin-bottom: 0.35em; }

  main strong { color: var(--navy); font-weight: 600; }
  main em { color: var(--slate); }

  main a {
    color: var(--cyan);
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(7, 159, 195, 0.3);
    transition: border-color 0.15s ease, color 0.15s ease;
  }

  main a:hover {
    color: var(--navy);
    border-color: var(--navy);
  }

  main code {
    background: var(--code-bg);
    color: var(--navy);
    padding: 2px 7px;
    border-radius: 4px;
    font-family: 'Cascadia Code', 'Consolas', 'SF Mono', monospace;
    font-size: 0.88em;
    border: 1px solid var(--code-border);
  }

  main pre {
    background: #F4F7FA;
    border: 1px solid var(--code-border);
    border-left: 4px solid var(--cyan);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 16px 20px;
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin: 1em 0;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  main pre code {
    background: transparent;
    border: none;
    padding: 0;
    color: var(--navy);
    font-size: 13.5px;
  }

  main blockquote {
    border-left: 4px solid var(--yellow);
    background: #FFFBF0;
    padding: 12px 20px;
    margin: 1em 0;
    color: var(--slate);
    border-radius: 0 6px 6px 0;
  }

  main blockquote p { margin-bottom: 0; }

  main table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    margin: 1.5em 0;
    background: white;
    border-radius: 8px;
    overflow: hidden;
    box-shadow: 0 1px 3px rgba(0, 57, 95, 0.06);
  }

  main thead {
    background: var(--navy);
    color: white;
  }

  main thead th {
    padding: 12px 16px;
    text-align: left;
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 14px;
    letter-spacing: 0.3px;
  }

  main tbody td {
    padding: 10px 16px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    font-size: 14.5px;
    color: #1F2D3D;
  }

  main tbody tr:nth-child(even) { background: #FAFBFC; }
  main tbody tr:hover { background: #F0F6FB; }

  main tbody tr:last-child td { border-bottom: none; }

  main hr {
    border: none;
    height: 1px;
    background: linear-gradient(90deg, transparent 0%, var(--border) 50%, transparent 100%);
    margin: 2.5em 0;
  }

  /* ─── Footer ─── */

  footer {
    background: var(--navy-dark);
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.7);
    padding: 24px 40px;
    text-align: center;
    font-size: 13px;
  }

  footer a {
    color: var(--cyan-light);
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px dotted rgba(74, 185, 212, 0.5);
  }

  footer a:hover { color: white; border-color: white; }

  /* ─── Responsive ─── */

  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .doc-hero { padding: 40px 24px 48px; }
    .doc-hero h1 { font-size: 36px; }
    main { padding: 32px 24px 56px; }
    main h1 { font-size: 26px; }
    main h2 { font-size: 20px; }
  }

  /* Print friendly */
  @media print {
    .doc-hero { background: var(--navy) !important; -webkit-print-color-adjust: exact; print-color-adjust: exact; }
    main { padding: 24px 0; max-width: 100%; }
    main pre { break-inside: avoid; }
    main table { break-inside: avoid; }
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>

<header class="doc-hero">
  <div class="doc-hero-inner">
    <div class="doc-eyebrow">
      <a href="index.html">← ERP Unite learning pack</a>
    </div>
    <h1>Application Architecture Guide</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">Layered architecture, CQRS without MediatR, EF Core + Unit of Work, the rules of the road</p>
  </div>
</header>

<main>
<h1>Application Architecture Guide</h1>
<h2>Purpose</h2>
<p>This document consolidates the current application architecture into a single, readable reference for architects and developers. It replaces the need to cross-read the original architecture notes and the refactoring changelog.</p>
<p>The goal is to describe the <strong>target architecture</strong> that teams should build against going forward, rather than preserving the chronological history of how the codebase got there.</p>
<hr />
<h2>1. Executive Summary</h2>
<p>The system follows a <strong>layered architecture</strong> with strict boundaries between:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Presentation layer</strong>: Blazor applications and shared UI libraries.</li>
<li><strong>Application layer</strong>: CQRS handlers, application models, and orchestration services.</li>
<li><strong>Persistence layer</strong>: Entity Framework Core, repositories, and Unit of Work.</li>
<li><strong>Domain/shared foundations</strong>: domain entities, enums, contracts, and shared utilities.</li>
</ol>
<p>The most important architectural decisions are:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Blazor components never talk directly to repositories or CQRS handlers.</strong> They use <strong>View Services</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>Reads and writes are separated with CQRS.</strong>
<ul>
<li>Reads go through <strong>Query Handlers</strong>.</li>
<li>Writes go through <strong>Command Handlers</strong>.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Query handlers use <code>IDbContextFactory</code> directly</strong> for efficient, no-tracking reads.</li>
<li><strong>Command handlers use <code>IUnitOfWork</code> + repositories</strong> for transactional writes.</li>
<li><strong>Repositories are intentionally simple</strong> and focused on reusable, single-entity data access.</li>
<li><strong>UI models and application models are different concepts</strong> and must remain separated.</li>
</ul>
<p>This architecture is designed to improve:</p>
<ul>
<li>clarity of responsibilities,</li>
<li>testability,</li>
<li>transaction safety,</li>
<li>performance for read-heavy flows,</li>
<li>consistency across multiple applications in the solution.</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>2. Architectural Goals</h2>
<p>The architecture optimizes for the following goals:</p>
<h3>2.1 Clear separation of concerns</h3>
<p>Each layer has one job and should not absorb responsibilities that belong elsewhere.</p>
<h3>2.2 Predictable dependency flow</h3>
<p>Dependencies should move inward and downward in a controlled way:</p>
<ul>
<li>Presentation depends on Application-facing abstractions such as View Services and ViewModels.</li>
<li>Application depends on Persistence abstractions and EF Core access patterns.</li>
<li>Persistence depends on EF Core and domain entities.</li>
</ul>
<h3>2.3 Fast and safe data access</h3>
<p>Read paths and write paths have different needs. The architecture treats them differently instead of forcing one generic pattern for everything.</p>
<h3>2.4 Refactor-friendly growth</h3>
<p>Because the solution contains multiple applications, the architecture emphasizes reusable patterns and conventions that can be replicated consistently.</p>
<hr />
<h2>3. Solution Structure</h2>
<p>A typical solution is organized like this:</p>
<pre><code class="language-text">Unite.Shared/
├── UniteHubCoreDb.Domain/              # Entities, enums, interfaces, domain contracts
├── UniteHubCoreDb.Persistence/         # EF Core access, repositories, UoW, configurations
│   ├── DbContexts/
│   ├── Repositories/
│   ├── Interfaces/
│   ├── UnitOfWork/
│   ├── Configurations/
│   └── Extensions/
├── Unite.Shared.Application/           # Commands, queries, models, services, DI extensions
│   ├── Commands/
│   ├── Queries/
│   ├── Models/
│   ├── Services/
│   └── Extensions/
├── Unite.Shared.Base/                  # Shared helpers and utilities
├── Unite.Shared.UI/                    # Shared UI artifacts, ViewModels, ViewServices
└── Tests/
    ├── UniteHubCoreDb.Persistence.Test.Unit/
    └── Unite.Shared.Application.Test.Unit/

{App}.BlazorApp/
├── Components/
│   ├── Pages/
│   ├── Shared/
│   └── Layout/
├── Services/                           # View Services
├── ViewModels/                         # UI-specific models
├── Validators/                         # FluentValidation / UI validation helpers
└── wwwroot/
</code></pre>
<h3>Technology stack</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Framework</strong>: .NET 10</li>
<li><strong>ORM</strong>: Entity Framework Core</li>
<li><strong>Database</strong>: SQL Server</li>
<li><strong>DI container</strong>: <code>Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection</code></li>
<li><strong>Testing</strong>: xUnit, Moq, EF Core InMemory</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>4. Layer Model and Dependency Rules</h2>
<h2>4.1 Layer overview</h2>
<pre><code class="language-text">Presentation Layer
  - Blazor applications
  - Shared UI libraries
  - Components, pages, layouts, ViewModels, validators, ViewServices

        ↓ uses

Application Layer
  - Query handlers
  - Command handlers
  - Application models / DTOs
  - Services and workflow orchestration

        ↓ uses

Persistence Layer
  - DbContext
  - Repositories
  - Unit of Work
  - EF configurations and query extensions
</code></pre>
<h2>4.2 Mandatory dependency rules</h2>
<h3>Presentation layer</h3>
<p><strong>Can depend on</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>View Services</li>
<li>ViewModels</li>
<li>Validators</li>
<li>UI framework concerns</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Must not depend on</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>repositories,</li>
<li><code>DbContext</code> / <code>IDbContextFactory</code>,</li>
<li>query handlers,</li>
<li>command handlers,</li>
<li>application DTOs/models directly.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Application layer</h3>
<p><strong>Can depend on</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>application models,</li>
<li>command/query contracts,</li>
<li><code>IUnitOfWork</code>,</li>
<li>repositories,</li>
<li><code>IDbContextFactory</code>,</li>
<li>domain types.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Must not depend on</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>UI components,</li>
<li>ViewModels,</li>
<li>display metadata,</li>
<li>form validation attributes.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Persistence layer</h3>
<p><strong>Can depend on</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>EF Core,</li>
<li>domain entities,</li>
<li>database configurations,</li>
<li>repository interfaces and implementations.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Must not contain</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>UI concerns,</li>
<li>DTO projections intended for the application layer,</li>
<li>business workflows,</li>
<li>presentation-specific validation.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Quick placement guide</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Concern</th>
<th>Belongs in</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Razor markup</td>
<td><code>.razor</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Component logic</td>
<td><code>.razor.cs</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Form validation attributes</td>
<td>ViewModel</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Read-only database queries</td>
<td>Query Handler</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Writes and transactions</td>
<td>Command Handler + Unit of Work</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Entity CRUD helpers</td>
<td>Repository</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>UI mapping</td>
<td>View Service</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Cross-operation orchestration</td>
<td>Application Service / Encapsulating Service</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>5. Presentation Layer (Blazor)</h2>
<h2>5.1 Design intent</h2>
<p>The Presentation layer is responsible for:</p>
<ul>
<li>rendering pages and components,</li>
<li>capturing user input,</li>
<li>invoking View Services,</li>
<li>showing validation and error messages,</li>
<li>managing UI state.</li>
</ul>
<p>It is <strong>not</strong> responsible for:</p>
<ul>
<li>business rules,</li>
<li>persistence logic,</li>
<li>composing EF queries,</li>
<li>transaction management.</li>
</ul>
<h2>5.2 Code-behind pattern</h2>
<p>Pages and components should follow a <strong>code-behind</strong> structure:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>{Component}.razor</code> contains markup only.</li>
<li><code>{Component}.razor.cs</code> contains C# logic only.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Why this matters</h3>
<p>This keeps components easier to read, test, and maintain. It also avoids mixing rendering concerns with service orchestration and event logic.</p>
<h3>Rule</h3>
<p>Do not place significant C# logic inside <code>@code</code> blocks when the component has non-trivial behavior.</p>
<h2>5.3 Component dependency boundary</h2>
<p>A component should normally inject only:</p>
<ul>
<li>a View Service,</li>
<li>framework services such as navigation or logging,</li>
<li>UI state helpers as needed.</li>
</ul>
<p>A component should never inject:</p>
<ul>
<li>a repository,</li>
<li><code>DbContext</code>,</li>
<li><code>IDbContextFactory</code>,</li>
<li>a command handler,</li>
<li>a query handler.</li>
</ul>
<h2>5.4 View Services</h2>
<p>View Services are the <strong>UI gateway</strong> into the Application layer.</p>
<p>They exist to:</p>
<ul>
<li>hide CQRS details from components,</li>
<li>map ViewModels to commands and queries,</li>
<li>map application results back into UI-friendly shapes,</li>
<li>translate command outcomes into UI-friendly result objects,</li>
<li>centralize UI-specific logging and error handling.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Typical responsibilities of a View Service</h3>
<ul>
<li>Build a query from a filter ViewModel.</li>
<li>Call a query handler.</li>
<li>Map the result DTOs into list/detail ViewModels.</li>
<li>Build a command from a form ViewModel.</li>
<li>Call a command handler.</li>
<li>Convert the result into <code>ViewServiceResult</code> or <code>ViewServiceResult&lt;T&gt;</code>.</li>
</ul>
<h2>5.5 ViewModels</h2>
<p>ViewModels are <strong>UI-specific representations</strong> of data.</p>
<p>They may contain:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>DataAnnotations</code>,</li>
<li>display attributes,</li>
<li>formatting concerns,</li>
<li>UI shaping, such as flattened strings or paging fields.</li>
</ul>
<p>They should not be reused as application DTOs.</p>
<h3>Common ViewModel categories</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Request ViewModels</strong>: form input models.</li>
<li><strong>Response ViewModels</strong>: display/grid/detail models.</li>
<li><strong>Filter ViewModels</strong>: search, paging, and filter criteria.</li>
</ul>
<h2>5.6 Validators</h2>
<p>Validation belongs in the UI layer when the validation is tied to:</p>
<ul>
<li>user-facing forms,</li>
<li>display labels,</li>
<li>field-level UX rules,</li>
<li>immediate interaction feedback.</li>
</ul>
<p>Complex domain validation that affects persistence still belongs in command handling or domain/application services.</p>
<h2>5.7 UI write result pattern</h2>
<p>View Services should return a UI-oriented result object for write operations.</p>
<p>Recommended shape:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>Success</code></li>
<li><code>ErrorMessage</code></li>
<li><code>ValidationErrors</code></li>
<li>optional <code>Value</code> for successful writes that return data</li>
</ul>
<p>This isolates the UI from the exact structure of command result objects.</p>
<hr />
<h2>6. Application Layer</h2>
<h2>6.1 Design intent</h2>
<p>The Application layer coordinates use cases.</p>
<p>It contains:</p>
<ul>
<li>Query Handlers,</li>
<li>Command Handlers,</li>
<li>Application Models / DTOs,</li>
<li>Services,</li>
<li>workflow orchestration,</li>
<li>cross-use-case policies.</li>
</ul>
<p>It does <strong>not</strong> own rendering or persistence mechanics directly.</p>
<h2>6.2 Simplified CQRS</h2>
<p>The solution uses <strong>CQRS without MediatR</strong>.</p>
<p>That means:</p>
<ul>
<li>handlers are regular injectable services,</li>
<li>there is no mediator pipeline by default,</li>
<li>command and query responsibilities are still kept separate,</li>
<li>reads and writes are optimized differently.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Why this approach was chosen</h3>
<p>It keeps the codebase simple while preserving the main benefits of CQRS:</p>
<ul>
<li>explicit intent,</li>
<li>clearer testing,</li>
<li>clear read/write separation,</li>
<li>better performance decisions for each path.</li>
</ul>
<h2>6.3 Query Handlers</h2>
<p>Use Query Handlers for:</p>
<ul>
<li>complex reads,</li>
<li>cross-entity joins,</li>
<li>aggregations,</li>
<li>projections into DTOs,</li>
<li>retrieval rules with business-aware filtering,</li>
<li>read scenarios that benefit from caching.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Query Handler rules</h3>
<ul>
<li>Use <code>IDbContextFactory&lt;UniteHubCoreDbContext&gt;</code>.</li>
<li>Create short-lived contexts per operation.</li>
<li>Use <code>AsNoTracking()</code> for read-only queries.</li>
<li>Prefer direct projection with <code>.Select(...)</code>.</li>
<li>Return DTOs or query result objects, not entities.</li>
<li>Throw exceptions for real failures; do not add <code>Success/Error</code> flags to normal query results.</li>
</ul>
<h3>When a query handler may use a repository</h3>
<p>A query handler may use a repository only for <strong>simple, reusable, single-entity lookups</strong>. It should not rely on repositories for the core of a complex read model.</p>
<h2>6.4 Command Handlers</h2>
<p>Use Command Handlers for:</p>
<ul>
<li>create/update/delete operations,</li>
<li>business validation before persistence,</li>
<li>write operations spanning multiple repositories,</li>
<li>transactional changes,</li>
<li>state transitions with database impact.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Command Handler rules</h3>
<ul>
<li>Use <code>IUnitOfWork</code>.</li>
<li>Retrieve repositories through the Unit of Work when inside a transaction.</li>
<li>Return <code>CommandResult</code> or <code>CommandResult&lt;T&gt;</code>.</li>
<li>Catch failures, roll back, and convert to a result.</li>
<li>Do not write directly with <code>DbContextFactory</code> in command handlers.</li>
</ul>
<h2>6.5 Application models / DTOs</h2>
<p>Application models are transport objects for the Application layer.</p>
<p>They should be:</p>
<ul>
<li>simple,</li>
<li>serializable-friendly,</li>
<li>free of UI validation attributes,</li>
<li>free of display metadata,</li>
<li>free of rendering logic.</li>
</ul>
<p>They may represent:</p>
<ul>
<li>command payloads,</li>
<li>query projections,</li>
<li>result containers,</li>
<li>cross-layer contracts between Application and UI-facing services.</li>
</ul>
<h2>6.6 Services in the Application layer</h2>
<p>Not every operation should become a repository or handler.</p>
<h3>Use a simple service when you need</h3>
<ul>
<li>a calculation,</li>
<li>a transformation,</li>
<li>a reusable utility,</li>
<li>a stateless rule evaluator.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Use an encapsulating service when you need</h3>
<ul>
<li>orchestration across multiple handlers,</li>
<li>caching across operations,</li>
<li>a multi-step workflow that is larger than one command or one query.</li>
</ul>
<p>Examples include security resolution, workflow orchestration, and combined read models built from more than one handler.</p>
<h2>6.7 Choosing the right construct</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Scenario</th>
<th>Recommended construct</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Get entity by ID</td>
<td>Repository or simple Query Handler</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Get entity list with simple filter</td>
<td>Repository</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Cross-entity join / aggregation</td>
<td>Query Handler</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Create or update data</td>
<td>Command Handler</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Transaction across multiple entities</td>
<td>Command Handler + UoW</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Pure calculation</td>
<td>Service</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Combine results from multiple handlers</td>
<td>Encapsulating Service</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Apply caching across multiple reads</td>
<td>Encapsulating Service</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>7. Persistence Layer</h2>
<h2>7.1 Design intent</h2>
<p>The Persistence layer provides reliable and reusable access to stored data.</p>
<p>It is responsible for:</p>
<ul>
<li>EF Core configuration,</li>
<li>entity mappings,</li>
<li>repositories,</li>
<li>Unit of Work,</li>
<li>low-level query extension utilities.</li>
</ul>
<p>It is not responsible for:</p>
<ul>
<li>application read models,</li>
<li>UI mapping,</li>
<li>business workflows,</li>
<li>use-case orchestration.</li>
</ul>
<h2>7.2 <code>IDbContextFactory</code> as the default access strategy</h2>
<p>The architecture prefers <code>IDbContextFactory&lt;UniteHubCoreDbContext&gt;</code> over injecting a long-lived <code>DbContext</code> directly.</p>
<h3>Benefits</h3>
<ul>
<li>short-lived contexts per operation,</li>
<li>safe parallel query execution,</li>
<li>cleaner behavior in Blazor Server,</li>
<li>fewer lifetime and tracking issues,</li>
<li>better separation between read flows and transactional write flows.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Context usage model</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Scenario</th>
<th>Strategy</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Read-only query</td>
<td>New context + <code>AsNoTracking()</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Independent write</td>
<td>New context and save</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Multiple related writes</td>
<td>Shared context via Unit of Work</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Explicit transaction</td>
<td>Unit of Work</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>8. Repository Pattern</h2>
<h2>8.1 Repository role</h2>
<p>Repositories provide <strong>simple, reusable, single-entity access</strong>.</p>
<p>They are meant to be a stable persistence abstraction, not a place where complex read-model logic accumulates.</p>
<h2>8.2 Abstract generic base repository</h2>
<p>Repositories inherit from an abstract <code>GenericRepository&lt;T&gt;</code>.</p>
<p>This base supports two operating modes.</p>
<h3>Factory mode</h3>
<p>Used for normal DI-driven repository usage.</p>
<ul>
<li>Constructor receives <code>IDbContextFactory&lt;UniteHubCoreDbContext&gt;</code>.</li>
<li>A new context is created per operation.</li>
<li>Best for independent work and parallel access.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Context mode</h3>
<p>Used by the Unit of Work.</p>
<ul>
<li>Constructor receives <code>UniteHubCoreDbContext</code>.</li>
<li>A shared context is used across operations.</li>
<li>Best for multi-step writes and transactions.</li>
</ul>
<h2>8.3 <code>ExecuteWithContextAsync</code></h2>
<p>Repository implementations should use a common helper such as <code>ExecuteWithContextAsync(...)</code>.</p>
<p>That helper:</p>
<ul>
<li>uses the Unit of Work context when one exists,</li>
<li>otherwise creates a short-lived context from the factory,</li>
<li>centralizes context lifetime behavior,</li>
<li>removes context management from public repository APIs.</li>
</ul>
<h2>8.4 Repository interface design</h2>
<p>Repository interfaces should expose <strong>entity-centric operations</strong> such as:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>ReadByIdAsync</code></li>
<li><code>ReadAsync(predicate)</code></li>
<li><code>ListAsync(predicate)</code></li>
<li><code>AddAsync</code></li>
<li><code>UpdateAsync</code></li>
<li><code>DeleteAsync</code></li>
<li>paging helpers where needed</li>
</ul>
<p>A key rule from the refactor is that <strong>repository methods should not accept <code>DbContext</code> parameters</strong>. Context selection is an internal implementation detail.</p>
<h2>8.5 Concrete repositories</h2>
<p>Each concrete repository should provide both constructors:</p>
<ol>
<li><code>IDbContextFactory&lt;UniteHubCoreDbContext&gt;</code> constructor for DI.</li>
<li><code>UniteHubCoreDbContext</code> constructor for Unit of Work usage.</li>
</ol>
<p>This makes a repository usable in both independent and transactional scenarios without duplicating repository types.</p>
<h2>8.6 What belongs in a repository</h2>
<p>Good repository responsibilities:</p>
<ul>
<li>simple single-entity lookup,</li>
<li>simple filtered list,</li>
<li>one-level include when still entity-centric,</li>
<li>reusable CRUD helpers,</li>
<li>bulk entity operations.</li>
</ul>
<p>Examples:</p>
<ul>
<li>get entity by external ID,</li>
<li>get active entities by a simple predicate,</li>
<li>insert or update entities,</li>
<li>get entity with one simple related navigation.</li>
</ul>
<h2>8.7 What does not belong in a repository</h2>
<p>Move the operation to a Query Handler when it involves:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>GroupBy</code>,</li>
<li><code>ToDictionary</code>,</li>
<li>3 or more joined entities,</li>
<li>DTO projections,</li>
<li>business-rule-heavy filtering,</li>
<li>calculations or aggregations,</li>
<li>caching,</li>
<li>many lines of specialized LINQ.</li>
</ul>
<h2>8.8 Repository design constraints</h2>
<p>Repositories should:</p>
<ul>
<li>return entities, not DTOs,</li>
<li>avoid <code>IQueryable</code> leakage,</li>
<li>materialize results internally,</li>
<li>remain reusable across handlers,</li>
<li>stay free of business workflows.</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>9. Unit of Work Pattern</h2>
<h2>9.1 Purpose</h2>
<p>The Unit of Work exists to coordinate <strong>multiple write operations as one transactional unit</strong>.</p>
<p>It ensures that related changes either:</p>
<ul>
<li>all commit together, or</li>
<li>all roll back together.</li>
</ul>
<h2>9.2 Responsibilities</h2>
<p>A Unit of Work should provide:</p>
<ul>
<li>access to the shared context when truly needed,</li>
<li><code>BeginTransactionAsync</code>,</li>
<li><code>CommitAsync</code>,</li>
<li><code>RollbackAsync</code>,</li>
<li><code>SaveChangesAsync</code>,</li>
<li>repository creation for the shared-context mode.</li>
</ul>
<h2>9.3 Concrete repository requirement</h2>
<p>Inside the Unit of Work, repositories are retrieved by <strong>concrete type</strong>, not interface type.</p>
<p>Example:</p>
<pre><code class="language-csharp">var repository = _unitOfWork.GetRepository&lt;PartyRepository&gt;();
</code></pre>
<h3>Why</h3>
<p>The implementation creates repositories directly against the shared context. It does not resolve them back through the DI container.</p>
<p>That design was kept because it is:</p>
<ul>
<li>simpler,</li>
<li>more explicit,</li>
<li>free of <code>IServiceProvider</code> plumbing,</li>
<li>compatible with cached constructor lookup.</li>
</ul>
<h2>9.4 Transaction guidance</h2>
<p>Use an explicit transaction when:</p>
<ul>
<li>multiple repositories are updated in one use case,</li>
<li>order of writes matters,</li>
<li>partial success would leave inconsistent data.</li>
</ul>
<p>Use <code>SaveChangesAsync()</code> without an explicit transaction only for truly simple, isolated writes where a broader transaction is unnecessary.</p>
<h2>9.5 Lifetime guidance</h2>
<ul>
<li>A Unit of Work should be <strong>scoped</strong>.</li>
<li>It should be <strong>short-lived</strong>.</li>
<li>It should not be stored across requests or long-running UI sessions.</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>10. Read/Write Flow Through the System</h2>
<h2>10.1 Read flow</h2>
<pre><code class="language-text">Blazor Component
  -&gt; View Service
  -&gt; Query Handler
  -&gt; IDbContextFactory / DbContext
  -&gt; SQL Server
  -&gt; Query Result / DTO
  -&gt; View Service maps to ViewModel
  -&gt; Component renders
</code></pre>
<h3>Read flow characteristics</h3>
<ul>
<li>optimized for projection,</li>
<li>no-tracking by default,</li>
<li>designed for complex querying when needed,</li>
<li>independent of transactional write concerns.</li>
</ul>
<h2>10.2 Write flow</h2>
<pre><code class="language-text">Blazor Component
  -&gt; View Service
  -&gt; Command Handler
  -&gt; IUnitOfWork
  -&gt; Repository / Repositories
  -&gt; Shared DbContext transaction
  -&gt; SQL Server
  -&gt; CommandResult
  -&gt; ViewServiceResult
  -&gt; Component updates UI
</code></pre>
<h3>Write flow characteristics</h3>
<ul>
<li>validation before persistence,</li>
<li>explicit transaction boundary when needed,</li>
<li>repository reuse for entity access,</li>
<li>rollback support on failure.</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>11. Dependency Injection Strategy</h2>
<h2>11.1 Recommended lifetimes</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Service type</th>
<th>Lifetime</th>
<th>Reason</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>IUnitOfWork</code></td>
<td>Scoped</td>
<td>one transactional scope per request / operation</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Repositories</td>
<td>Scoped</td>
<td>stateless entry points over data access</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Command Handlers</td>
<td>Scoped</td>
<td>request-scoped use-case execution</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Query Handlers</td>
<td>Scoped</td>
<td>request-scoped use-case execution</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>IDbContextFactory</code></td>
<td>Singleton</td>
<td>thread-safe factory</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Application services</td>
<td>Scoped</td>
<td>consistent composition scope</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>View Services</td>
<td>Scoped</td>
<td>UI-facing orchestration per request/circuit scope</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h2>11.2 Registration structure</h2>
<p>A clean composition root should register services in clear groups:</p>
<ul>
<li>DbContext factory registration,</li>
<li>repositories,</li>
<li>Unit of Work,</li>
<li>CQRS handlers,</li>
<li>application services,</li>
<li>View Services.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Typical registration shape</h3>
<pre><code class="language-csharp">services.AddDbContextFactory&lt;UniteHubCoreDbContext&gt;(options =&gt;
    options.UseSqlServer(connectionString));

services.AddScoped&lt;IUnitOfWork, UnitOfWork&gt;();
services.AddScoped&lt;IPartyRepository, PartyRepository&gt;();
services.AddScoped&lt;IUserRoleRepository, UserRoleRepository&gt;();

services.AddScoped&lt;ICommandHandler&lt;CreatePartyCommand, CommandResult&lt;Guid&gt;&gt;, CreatePartyCommandHandler&gt;();
services.AddScoped&lt;IQueryHandler&lt;GetPartyByIdQuery, GetPartyByIdQueryResult&gt;, GetPartyByIdQueryHandler&gt;();

services.AddScoped&lt;IPartyViewService, PartyViewService&gt;();
</code></pre>
<h2>11.3 Injection rules in Blazor</h2>
<p>If a component uses code-behind, keep injections in the <code>.razor.cs</code> file.
Do not split injections between <code>.razor</code> and <code>.razor.cs</code> for the same component.</p>
<hr />
<h2>12. DTO and ViewModel Separation</h2>
<h2>12.1 Why the separation exists</h2>
<p>The architecture deliberately separates:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Application DTOs / Models</strong> for use cases and handler results,</li>
<li><strong>ViewModels</strong> for rendering, input, and validation.</li>
</ul>
<p>This prevents UI concerns from leaking into application contracts and prevents application contracts from becoming overloaded with display behavior.</p>
<h2>12.2 Application DTO characteristics</h2>
<p>Application DTOs should:</p>
<ul>
<li>represent use-case data,</li>
<li>be UI-agnostic,</li>
<li>avoid validation attributes,</li>
<li>avoid display labels and formatting annotations.</li>
</ul>
<h2>12.3 ViewModel characteristics</h2>
<p>ViewModels should:</p>
<ul>
<li>express UI semantics,</li>
<li>include validation metadata,</li>
<li>include display metadata,</li>
<li>reshape data for component needs,</li>
<li>support grid/detail/form scenarios.</li>
</ul>
<h2>12.4 Mapping location</h2>
<p>Mapping between DTOs and ViewModels belongs in <strong>View Services</strong>.</p>
<p>That is the correct place to:</p>
<ul>
<li>flatten or reshape values for the UI,</li>
<li>combine fields for display,</li>
<li>convert form input into commands,</li>
<li>adapt command/query results to pages and dialogs.</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>13. Quality, Performance, and Maintainability Rules</h2>
<h2>13.1 Performance rules</h2>
<ul>
<li>Use <code>AsNoTracking()</code> for read-only queries.</li>
<li>Project directly to DTOs in query handlers.</li>
<li>Avoid loading full entities when a read model only needs selected columns.</li>
<li>Avoid <code>IQueryable</code> leakage from repositories.</li>
<li>Avoid N+1 query behavior by designing query handlers intentionally.</li>
<li>Batch where appropriate.</li>
<li>Apply caching at the service level, not inside repositories.</li>
</ul>
<h2>13.2 Maintainability rules</h2>
<ul>
<li>Keep repositories simple and reusable.</li>
<li>Keep services single-purpose unless they are explicitly orchestration services.</li>
<li>Avoid “god services” that mix reads, writes, mapping, validation, and persistence.</li>
<li>Keep layer boundaries explicit even if a shortcut seems convenient.</li>
<li>Prefer feature-oriented organization in Commands and Queries.</li>
</ul>
<h2>13.3 Error-handling rules</h2>
<ul>
<li>Queries should return data shapes; failures should normally surface as exceptions.</li>
<li>Commands should convert expected business failures into <code>CommandResult</code> values.</li>
<li>View Services should convert command outcomes into UI-friendly result objects.</li>
<li>Transactional command failures must roll back explicitly.</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>14. Testing Strategy by Layer</h2>
<h2>14.1 Query Handlers</h2>
<ul>
<li>Prefer integration-style tests with EF Core InMemory or equivalent test infrastructure.</li>
<li>Verify projections, filters, and aggregation logic independently.</li>
</ul>
<h2>14.2 Command Handlers</h2>
<ul>
<li>Mock repositories and Unit of Work where appropriate.</li>
<li>Verify success, validation failures, and rollback behavior.</li>
</ul>
<h2>14.3 Repositories</h2>
<ul>
<li>Test repository methods with isolated in-memory databases.</li>
<li>Verify both read and write behavior.</li>
<li>For abstract repository bases, create concrete test implementations.</li>
</ul>
<h2>14.4 View Services</h2>
<ul>
<li>Mock handlers.</li>
<li>Verify mapping, result translation, and UI-facing error handling.</li>
</ul>
<p>The broader rule is that each architectural building block should be testable in isolation because responsibilities are clearly separated.</p>
<hr />
<h2>15. Architecture Checklist for New Features</h2>
<p>When implementing a new feature, use this checklist:</p>
<h3>UI</h3>
<ul>
<li>Create or update ViewModels.</li>
<li>Add validators if the feature introduces or changes forms.</li>
<li>Keep page logic in <code>.razor.cs</code>.</li>
<li>Call only View Services from components.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Application</h3>
<ul>
<li>Add a Query Handler for new complex reads.</li>
<li>Add a Command Handler for new writes.</li>
<li>Use DTOs or result records that are UI-agnostic.</li>
<li>Add an encapsulating service only if the workflow spans multiple handlers.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Persistence</h3>
<ul>
<li>Add or extend a repository only for reusable, entity-centric access.</li>
<li>Keep repository methods simple.</li>
<li>Use the dual-constructor pattern for new repositories.</li>
<li>Use Unit of Work for multi-step writes.</li>
</ul>
<h3>DI</h3>
<ul>
<li>Register repositories, handlers, services, and View Services explicitly.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Testing</h3>
<ul>
<li>Add tests at the same architectural level as the change.</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>16. Recommended Team Conventions</h2>
<p>The following conventions keep the architecture easy to navigate across applications:</p>
<ul>
<li>Organize Commands and Queries by feature.</li>
<li>Keep naming explicit: <code>CreateXxxCommandHandler</code>, <code>GetXxxQueryHandler</code>, <code>XxxViewService</code>, <code>XxxViewModel</code>.</li>
<li>Use XML documentation on public interfaces and important public services.</li>
<li>Keep code examples and templates aligned across applications.</li>
<li>Treat architecture rules as solution-wide standards, not per-application preferences.</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>17. Glossary</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Term</th>
<th>Meaning</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>CQRS</td>
<td>Separation of read operations and write operations</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Query Handler</td>
<td>Application component responsible for read use cases</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Command Handler</td>
<td>Application component responsible for write use cases</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Repository</td>
<td>Persistence abstraction for simple entity-centric data access</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Unit of Work</td>
<td>Transaction boundary coordinating multiple repository operations</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>View Service</td>
<td>UI-facing service that bridges Blazor components and application handlers</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>ViewModel</td>
<td>UI-specific model used for input, display, filtering, and validation</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>DTO / Application Model</td>
<td>Application-layer data contract used in commands, queries, and results</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>18. Final Guidance</h2>
<p>This architecture works best when teams resist the temptation to bypass the boundaries for convenience.</p>
<p>The core rule is simple:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>UI talks to View Services.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Reads go through Query Handlers.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Writes go through Command Handlers.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Repositories stay simple.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Transactions go through Unit of Work.</strong></li>
</ul>
<p>If a new requirement does not fit neatly into one of those categories, the answer is usually not to blur the layers. The answer is to introduce the correct abstraction in the correct layer.</p>

